<section class="wrap small">
  <h2>Small enough to forget about</h2>
  <p class="lead">The server is one Rust binary with no runtime and almost no dependencies. It needs only a few megabytes of memory, and the container image is about 1.5 MB. It sits happily on a Raspberry Pi next to everything else.</p>
  <p>Your data stays on your server. Tablets hold only a revocable token for their own tiles, and your smart home keys never leave the server.</p>
</section>

<section class="band start">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Up and running in three steps</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><h3>Start the server</h3><pre><code>docker run -d --name oldpad -p 8080:8080 \
  -v oldpad-data:/data \
  ghcr.io/bisand/oldpad:latest</code></pre></li>
      <li><h3>Create your passkey</h3><p>Run <code>docker logs oldpad</code> for the setup code, then open <code>/admin</code> and add the demo house or your own system.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Pair the tablet</h3><p>Open the address in Safari, scan the code it shows, and add it to the home screen.</p></li>
    </ol>
    <p><a class="btn main" href="/guide/install/">Read the install guide</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap join">
  <h2>Help more old devices come back</h2>
  <p class="lead">oldpad is young and open to help. Test it on a device we haven't tried, translate it into your language, or connect the system you use at home. A new integration is one Rust file, and the setup wizard builds itself.</p>
  <p class="actions"><a class="btn main" href="/contribute/">See how to contribute</a> <a class="btn" href="https://github.com/bisand/oldpad/issues">Open issues</a></p>
</section>
